All dominant modes achieve $\geq$3/5 model consistency; scopal and authorization scope are unanimous (5/5).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\% (GPT-4.1 and Claude 10--11\%; Qwen3 and DeepSeek 0\%; $p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}, extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Systematic nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. Prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) is type-dependent: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, inverted: agents exhibit \emph{convergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---\emph{systematic nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. 77.0\% of convergent violations reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}).

